a=s.index('<h3 id="s42">');b=s.index('<h3 id="s43">',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''<h3 id="s42">4.2. Spatial formulations and the continuous reference</h3>
<p>We compare the integrable semi-discretization with centered finite differences on a fixed physical grid, denoted here by integrable SD and FD. Both calculations begin with the continuous exact initial fields and use RK4 with \\(\Delta T=5\times10^{-4}\\). This comparison examines how the two spatial formulations approximate the same continuous wave family.</p>
<p>Tables 1 and 2 report terminal errors at \\(T=0.25\\) for three resolutions. The SD reciprocal spacing \\(a\\) and the FD physical spacing \\(\Delta x\\) take the same nominal values, giving the same node count at each resolution. SD density errors use exact cell averages, whereas FD density errors use exact nodal values, as specified in (16). Figures 3 and 4 show the corresponding midpoint fields and errors at \\(T=0.125\\). Halving the time step at spacing 0.02 changes the evolved states by less than \\(3\times10^{-14}\\), placing these spatial errors well above the observed temporal refinement difference.</p>
{{FIG_SPATIAL}}
<p>The two soliton cases show the same overall pattern: FD produces smaller errors in the reported field metrics, and its advantage increases under spatial refinement. The observed orders explain this trend. Integrable SD gives approximately first-order error reduction, while FD gives approximately second-order reduction. Halving the spacing therefore reduces the SD errors by about two and the FD errors by about four. The error coefficients and wave profile determine the absolute magnitudes within this pattern.</p>
<p>This difference in order is consistent with the analytical structure of the spatial approximations. The exact lattice phase expansion following (7) has a leading correction proportional to \\(a\\), and the SD slope equation contains the density-dependent first-order correction identified in (10). The centered differences used by FD have second-order local truncation errors for smooth fields. These properties help explain the refinement rates measured in Tables 1 and 2. Within SD, the velocity is reconstructed by accumulating jumps from the left boundary, so changes in the slope evolution can contribute to discrepancies farther along the chain. This is consistent with the downstream velocity errors seen in both figures, while the density responds through the changing cell widths.</p>
<p>The comparison thus relates the field errors to the approximation order and reconstruction of each spatial formulation. For the smooth profiles and resolutions examined here, the second-order FD approximation resolves the continuous fields more accurately than the first-order integrable lattice. The fixed and moving FD mesh choices are examined separately in Section 4.4.</p>
'''.replace('\\\\','\\')+s[b:]
s=s.replace('Tables 4 and 5','Tables 3 and 4')
a=s.index('<p>We now hold the direct moving FD equations fixed');b=s.index('{{FIG_MESH_EVOLUTION}}',a)
s=s[:a]+r'''<p>We now compare fixed FD and moving FD under RK4, and then examine the effect of curvature-adapted initial labels within moving FD. All calculations use the same continuous wave family, 201 nodes, time step \\(\Delta T=5\times10^{-4}\\) and terminal time \\(T=0.25\\). The fixed grid occupies \\([-4,4]\\); the moving grids use reciprocal endpoints \\(X=-4,4\\), with either uniform labels or the monitor in (15). Boundary closures remain those in Section 3.3.</p>
<p>To compare the fields at the same physical locations, we reconstruct every solution on 2,001 common points in \\([-2.5,2.5]\\). Fixed-grid fields are interpolated from their nodes; moving-grid velocity is interpolated from nodes and density from cell centers. The errors below compare these reconstructions with the continuous point fields. The fixed-versus-moving comparison therefore includes their spatial representations and boundary settings, while the comparison between the two moving grids isolates the initial label allocation under the same evolution equations.</p>
'''.replace('\\\\','\\')+s[b:]
